    idealAnswer: 'During autoregressive generation, past Keys and Values must be stored in GPU VRAM (the KV Cache) to avoid recomputing past tokens.\n\nFlaws of Traditional Serving:\nTraditional systems pre-allocate contiguous memory chunks for maximum context length (e.g. 8k tokens) per request. Because request lengths vary, 60–80% of KV cache VRAM is wasted due to internal and external memory fragmentation, severely capping batch size.\n\nPagedAttention (Kwon et al. / vLLM):\nInspired by OS virtual memory paging, PagedAttention partitions the KV cache into fixed-size physical blocks (e.g. 16 tokens per block) allocated dynamically as generation proceeds. Physical blocks do not need to be contiguous. Achieves near-zero (<4%) memory waste, unlocking 2–4x higher serving throughput.',
    keyPoints: ['Contiguous memory allocation causes 60-80% VRAM waste', 'PagedAttention treats KV cache like virtual memory pages', 'Enables 2-4x higher concurrency and throughput in vLLM'],
