    interviewerPrompt: 'Differentiate Data Drift (Covariate Shift), Concept Drift, and Prior Probability Shift. How does Population Stability Index (PSI) quantify drift?',
    idealAnswer: '• Data Drift (Covariate Shift): P(X) changes while P(Y | X) remains constant. (e.g., app marketing redesign attracts a different demographic).\n• Concept Drift: P(Y | X) changes while P(X) might stay identical. (e.g., macroeconomic inflation causes historically safe debt-to-income ratios to default at 3x higher rates).\n• Prior Probability Shift: P(Y) changes (e.g. seasonal spike in holiday purchase frequency).\n\nPopulation Stability Index (PSI):\n  PSI = Σ (Actual% - Expected%) * ln(Actual% / Expected%)\nInterpretation:\n• PSI < 0.1: No significant distribution change.\n• PSI 0.1–0.2: Moderate drift; schedule inspection.\n• PSI > 0.2: Significant drift; triggers automated retraining pipeline.',
    keyPoints: ['Data drift: P(X) shifts; Concept drift: relationship P(Y|X) shifts', 'PSI quantifies divergence between reference and live population', 'PSI > 0.2 triggers automated retraining alerts'],
    expectedConcepts: ['Data Drift', 'Concept Drift', 'Covariate Shift', 'Population Stability Index (PSI)'],
    relatedTopicId: 'mlops_model-serving-and-monitoring'
